export anchor PluginRuntime:
    description:
        A plugin's command runs in QuickJS, embedded in the compiler through
        the rquickjs crate, and its TypeScript is turned into JavaScript by
        oxc, in the compiler too. Nothing else has to be installed: no Node,
        no tsc, and no build step for a plugin's author.

    transpiling:
        Each module is transpiled as it is imported: oxc parses it as
        TypeScript, removes everything that is only types, and prints the
        JavaScript, which QuickJS runs as it is, without lowering it to an
        older version. A .js or .mjs file is used as it is. As in TypeScript,
        an import nothing uses as a value is removed.

        A syntax error stops the command with the file and line it is on.

    modules:
        A plugin imports @piton/plugin and its own files, by a relative path,
        like ./tags. A path without an extension is tried as it is, then
        with .ts, then .js, then as a directory's index.ts. Nothing else can
        be imported, a path leading outside the plugin's directory included,
        so a plugin is what its directory holds.

    oneCommandOneRuntime:
        Each command invoked starts a runtime of its own and ends it when the
        command is done, so nothing a plugin keeps lasts from one command to
        the next.

    eventLoop:
        JavaScript runs on one thread. What a plugin waits for, like a line
        from a process or a timer, is gathered while it waits and handed back
        on that thread, resolving the promise that was waiting for it. So a
        plugin running many processes at once writes plain async code, and
        never has two pieces of its JavaScript running at the same time.

        A command is done when its handler's promise settles. If it is still
        waiting with nothing left that could ever answer, it fails, saying it
        waited for something that can never happen, rather than hanging.

    ending:
        However a command ends, every process it started that is still
        running is stopped, and every temporary file it wrote is removed.

    failures:
        A plugin that throws, or doesn't load, fails its command: the error
        says which plugin and what went wrong, with the JavaScript stack when
        there is one, and the exit code is 1.
